# ==============================================================================
# Build-context exclusions for BOTH images.
#
# One file, because both builds use the repo root as their context: `web/` imports
# `../shared/*.ts` (web/src/app/page.tsx, web/next.config.ts and two more), so it
# cannot be built from `./web` — see Dockerfile.web's header.
#
# THIS FILE IS A SECURITY CONTROL, NOT HYGIENE. `.env` holds a live OPENAI_API_KEY
# and the real TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN, and it sits in the context. Without the first rule
# below, `COPY server ./server` is fine but any `COPY . .` bakes both into an image
# layer, where they survive deletion in a later layer and are readable by anyone who
# can `docker save` the image. The container gets its secrets from compose at RUN
# time instead, which is why `--env-file-if-exists=.env` in the CMD is a deliberate
# no-op inside the image.
# ==============================================================================

# --- secrets ---
.env
.env.*

# --- host build artifacts that would shadow the ones the image builds ---
# node_modules is the dangerous one: these are macOS/arm64 installs, and copying them
# over a linux stage's own install produces a container that boots and then fails on
# the first native call. `**/` because plain `node_modules` matches only the root and
# web/ has its own install tree.
**/node_modules
**/.next
dist/
coverage/

# --- not needed to build or run ---
.git
.gitignore
docs
tests
vitest.config.ts
.playwright-mcp
.superpowers
langfuse-data
docker-compose.langfuse.yml
*.log
.DS_Store

# ==============================================================================
# DO NOT ADD, however tempting — each of these breaks the build in a way whose
# error message does not name the file:
#
#   .npmrc, web/.npmrc          pnpm does NOT walk up for registry config (.npmrc:8),
#                               so web/ needs its own copy. Drop either and pnpm
#                               resolves against whatever registry the machine's user
#                               config names, producing an unreproducible install.
#   pnpm-workspace.yaml,        carries the `allowBuilds` decision. pnpm 11 FAILS the
#   web/pnpm-workspace.yaml     install with ERR_PNPM_IGNORED_BUILDS when it is
#                               undecided — so an absent file is not a default, it is
#                               a hard stop.
#   pnpm-lock.yaml              --frozen-lockfile has nothing to freeze against.
# ==============================================================================
